{{/pagedOperation}}
{{/hasPagedOperation}}

The cursor you pass to the method is the starting position, and the next cursor of a page resumes the list: pass it as the starting cursor of a new call. The page size and every other parameter, option and header are repeated unchanged on each page request, and each page is an ordinary call with its own retries, timeout, redirects, authentication and redaction. An error on a later page is raised from the read that needed it, after the items of the earlier pages were delivered, and ends the iteration; the SDK never skips a page. A list ends when the next cursor is absent, `null` or empty, and an empty page with a next cursor continues. A page that returns the cursor that was just sent raises `PaginationException`, a direct child of `{{errorPrefix}}Exception`, after its items were delivered, instead of looping. There is no total limit and no collect-all: use `Stream.limit`.

A list that is followed by a `Link` header (`rel="next"`, RFC 8288) takes the next URL from the header of each response. A next URL that leaves the scheme, host or port of the client is refused with `PaginationException` and nothing, no credentials either, is sent to it; such a method has no starting cursor, so it cannot resume.

The generator finds a paged operation by its names: a GET with one JSON 2xx response that has one array property, and either one cursor query parameter among `cursor`, `after`, `page_token`, `next_token`, `continuation_token` and `marker` paired with one next-cursor property among `next_cursor`, `next_page_token`, `next_token`, `next_marker`, `next_continuation_token`, `continuation_token` and `next` of the same type, or a `Link` header declared on the response. An operation that is ambiguous, or is streamed, keeps only its plain method, and the generation log names it and says why. The `x-fern-pagination` and `x-speakeasy-pagination` (cursor type) extensions are read as hints. The generator option `paginationOverrides` (a JSON object that maps an operation id to `none` or to `{style, requestParam, responseProperty, itemsProperty}`) wins over a hint and over inference, and `pagination=off` switches the feature off.

### Custom HTTP client

`TransportOptions.builder().httpClient(client)` makes the SDK send every
request through your own `java.net.http.HttpClient`. The SDK keeps its request
timeout, user agent and redirect handling on top and never closes the
client; you own its lifecycle. Because the client carries its own proxy and TLS
settings, combining it with `proxy`, `caCertPath` or `verifySsl(false)` fails
at `build()`, as does a client whose redirect policy is not `Redirect.NEVER`.

### Testing your code

Every API group has an interface (`{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{classname}}Interface{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}`) and the client has one (`{{clientClassName}}Interface`), so a test can replace the SDK with a hand-written fake: no mocking library, no network.

```java file
{{#apiInfo}}
{{#apis}}
{{#operations}}
import {{apiPackage}}.{{classname}}Interface;
{{/operations}}
{{/apis}}
{{/apiInfo}}
import {{invokerPackage}}.{{clientClassName}}Interface;
import java.lang.reflect.Proxy;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

final class FakeExample {
    // Code under test takes the group interface, never the generated class.
    static void use({{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{classname}}Interface{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}} api) {}

    // A fake is any implementation of the interface; this one records every call it receives.
    static <T> T fake(Class<T> type, List<String> calls) {
        return type.cast(Proxy.newProxyInstance(
                type.getClassLoader(),
                new Class<?>[] {type},
                (proxy, method, arguments) -> {
                    calls.add(method.getName());
                    return null;
                }));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> calls = new ArrayList<>();
        use(fake({{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{classname}}Interface{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}.class, calls));

        {{clientClassName}}Interface client = new {{clientClassName}}Interface() {
{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#operations}}            @Override
            public {{classname}}Interface get{{clientAccessorSuffix}}() {
                return fake({{classname}}Interface.class, calls);
            }

{{/operations}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}            @Override
            public void close() {}
        };
        client.close();
        System.out.println(calls);
    }
}
```

A new operation in the API adds a method to the interface, so a fake that implements the whole interface stops compiling after you regenerate; delegate to a base fake or use a proxy like this one, so only the operation you fake is written.

### Per-call options

Every operation takes an optional trailing options object named after it
(`FindItemsOptions` and the like), even an operation that has no query, header,
form or cookie parameter and no security. Besides the operation's own optional
parameters, it accepts three per-call settings that apply to that one call only:
`timeout(millis)` replaces the transport's end-to-end timeout, `maxRetries(n)`
replaces its retry count (`0` turns retries off), and `headers(map)` adds
request headers after the default headers; authentication headers are applied
last and still win. Leave the options object out, or pass `null`, to use the
client's settings. An options object carries no authenticator: the credential comes
from the client, as described next.

### Hooks and telemetry

One optional hook runs around every operation call. It can edit the request headers before the send and observe the status or the error afterwards, and it does nothing when none is set. It runs once per call, however many sends happen inside it (retries, the one replay after a `401`), once for the plain and the `WithHttpInfo` variant alike, and also when you supply your own transport.

```java statements
import {{invokerPackage}}.CallHook;
import {{invokerPackage}}.{{clientClassName}};

CallHook hook = (info, headers) -> {
    headers.put("X-Caller", "billing");
    long started = System.nanoTime();
    return outcome -> System.out.println(
            info.operationId() + " " + outcome.statusCode() + " "
                    + (System.nanoTime() - started) / 1_000_000 + " ms");
};
{{clientClassName}} client = {{clientClassName}}.builder("https://api.example.com")
        .callHook(hook)
        .build();
```

`start(info, headers)` runs after the headers are final (defaults, operation headers, per-call headers, authentication, cookies and the propagated trace context), so it can read and replace any of them; reuse the spelling a header already has. A header it sets is on every send of the call. `info` carries `operationId` (as written in the spec), `method`, `urlTemplate`, `url` (without query string and fragment) and `serverAddress`. It returns the observer to call when the call ends, or `null`. `finish(outcome)` runs exactly once on every way the call can end, with `statusCode` (`0` when no response arrived), the lower-cased response `headers` (`null` when no response arrived) and the `error` the caller receives (`null` on success). An error thrown by `start` reaches the caller unchanged, nothing is sent and `finish` is not called; an error thrown by `finish` is swallowed. The hook never changes the result or the error of the call. Combine several hooks with `CallHook.chain(first, second)`: they start in the order given and finish in the reverse order.

`OpenTelemetryCallHook` is the hook that records one span, one request count and one duration histogram per call with the OpenTelemetry API. Add `opentelemetry-api` to your build (the SDK itself does not need it), then pass `OpenTelemetryCallHook.create()` for the global instance or `OpenTelemetryCallHook.create(openTelemetry)` for your own.

```java statements
import {{invokerPackage}}.{{clientClassName}};
import {{invokerPackage}}.OpenTelemetryCallHook;

{{clientClassName}} client = {{clientClassName}}.builder("https://api.example.com")
        .callHook(OpenTelemetryCallHook.create())
        .build();
```

The span is named with the operation id, has kind client and carries `http.request.method`, `url.template`, `server.address`, `http.response.status_code` (only when a response arrived) and `error.type` (only on failure: the status code for a status of 400 or more, otherwise `timeout`, `network_error`, `serialization_error`, `cancelled` or `_OTHER`). The counter `http.client.request.count` (unit `{request}`) and the histogram `http.client.request.duration` (unit `s`) carry the same attributes; the histogram's own count equals the request count. The instrumentation scope is the published package name and the SDK version. The hook writes `traceparent` itself, so it names the client span as its parent. No attribute carries a query string, a header value or a credential.

{{#hasClientSchemes}}
### Credentials per security scheme

`{{clientClassName}}.builder(host)` takes one optional credential per security scheme,
named after the scheme, so a client can hold several at once:

{{>credentials_per_scheme}}

Each operation uses the credential of the first scheme in its own security list that
the client holds. When it holds none of them, the operation falls back to the single
authenticator of `new {{clientClassName}}(authenticator)` if there is one, and is sent
without a credential otherwise. An operation that declares `security: []` never sends one.
There is no per-call override of the scheme.

{{/hasClientSchemes}}
### Cookie parameters

A cookie parameter is sent exactly as you give it when every character is a legal RFC 6265
cookie-octet (a `%` is one, so a value you percent-encoded yourself goes out unchanged). A
value with a space, double quote, comma, semicolon, backslash, control character or
non-ASCII character fails with `IllegalArgumentException` before any request is sent;
percent-encode it first. The SDK never encodes a cookie value for you.

{{#hasSecuritySchemeEnvVars}}
### Credentials from the environment

The authenticator generated for each security scheme reads a credential you leave
out from an environment variable, once, when the authenticator is built. A value
you pass always wins; a `null` or empty value (and an empty variable) counts as
not set. A credential found in neither place fails the constructor with an
`IllegalArgumentException` that names the variable. A token that rotates comes
from a callback instead: `new BearerAuthenticator(host, () -> currentToken())`
runs it before every request (passing both a token and a callback is refused).
The variables are:

| Scheme | Credential | Variable |
| --- | --- | --- |
{{#securitySchemeEnvVars}}
| `{{schemeId}}` | `{{field}}` | `{{envName}}` |
{{/securitySchemeEnvVars}}

The prefix `{{envVarPrefix}}` comes from the `envVarPrefix` generator option, or
the API title when the option is not set. The general `BearerAuthenticator`,
`BasicAuthenticator` and `ApiKeyAuthenticator` classes never read the environment.

{{/hasSecuritySchemeEnvVars}}

### Errors

A failed call throws a subclass of `ApiException`. Besides `getStatusCode()`,
`getResponseHeaders()` and `getResponseBody()`, it carries `getRequestMethod()`
and `getRequestPath()` (the path without its query string), and `toString()`
renders them on a `Request: GET /items/1` line, so a log line names the call
without the URL's query string, which may hold a credential.

### Models are immutable

A model's fields are `final`. Build one with `Model.builder()` (or its
constructor when it has required properties) and copy it with one property
changed through `model.toBuilder().property(value).build()`. A required property
that is missing fails when the model is built, whether from code or from JSON.
Collections are copied into unmodifiable ones. A model that accepts extra keys
exposes them read-only through `getAdditionalProperties()` and writes them with
the builder's `putAdditionalProperty`, `putAllAdditionalProperties`,
`removeAdditionalProperty` and `additionalProperties(map)`.

### Unions

A `oneOf` or `anyOf` model has a typed factory per variant (`Union.ofVariant(v)`)
and, for a variant that is a plain class, `isVariant()` and `asVariant()`
(an `Optional`). An operation with several `2xx` responses decodes the body as
the schema of the status that arrived and returns the union.

